# a waiter holding a reserved job half-closes: it gets TIMED_OUT, and the
# server then reads EOF on the connection and closes it, which releases the job.
# The job state is only checked after the server-side close: between sending
# TIMED_OUT and handling the pending EOF, the job is still reserved, but whether
# another client's command lands inside that window is a race inside the
# reference (it flips under load), so it is not observed here. A pause follows
# the close: the client sees the close before the server has processed the
# disconnect that releases the job (in a cluster, before that disconnect has
# been committed), and the polling a state check would need does not exist in
# the DSL.
@p send "put 0 0 60 1\r\nx\r\n"
@p recv
@h send "reserve\r\n"
@h recv
@h send "reserve\r\n"
sleep 100ms
@h shutdown_write
@h recv
@h recv_closed 2s
sleep 300ms
@p send "stats-job 1\r\n"
@p recv
